

                    Star Battles Scenario Creation Utility

                               Instructions

                             by Chris Nystrom

                                (c) 1990


      It is easy to create your own Star Battles scenarios. Simply type
SBCREATE and follow the prompts.
   First you will be given a generated stardate, or you can give your own.
   Then you will be asked to input the galaxy size from 10 to 70. This
determines the size of demensions of the quadrant grid. Remember each quadrant
is a 10 X 10 grid in itself, so you can create a very large galaxy.
   You will then be asked to input the number of fleets. This is how the ships
are divided. You should not have more than 20 ships in a fleet.
   Last you will be asked the total number of ships in the scenario. The more
ships you have the slower the game will process moves.
   The program will then create the Galaxy based on the parameters that you
set, and all the stars will be created and placed into the galaxy.
   You will then be asked for the names of each of the fleets. It can be up to
60 characters.
   You will then see the ship number, and be asked for what side the ship will
be on. The side number determines enemies from friends. Ships controlled by
the computer will attack other ships if the side number is different. Ships
can be of different cultures, and have the same side number (allies), or ships
can be of the same culture and have different side numbers (a rebellion).
   Next you will be presented with the known races of the galaxy:

      1.   Federation
      2.   Klargon
      3.   Zornian
      4.   Hasmurd
      5.   Robotron
      6.   Farnic
      7.   Chaldean
      8.   Phesian
      9.   Hattian
      10.  Alien
      11.  Other

   If you choose other you will be asked to input the name of a culture, up to
10 characters.
   You will then be asked to input the fleet number, based on the number of
fleets you previously entered. After you are done an abreviated version of the
fleet name will appear.
   You will be asked to enter the ship type from the list of 22 ships. They
are:

      1.   Patrol
      2.   Corvette
      3.   Torpedo Craft
      4.   Frigate
      5.   Destroyer
      6.   Light Cruiser
      7.   Heavy Cruiser
      8.   Monitor
      9.   BattleCruiser
      10.  Battle Ship
      11.  Dreadnought
      12.  DeathStar
      13.  Space Station
      14.  Space Outpost
      15.  Space Dock
      16.  Sensor Ship
      17.  Fueler
      18.  Repair Ship
      19.  Freighter
      20.  Transport
      21.  Tanker
      22.  Barge

   After giving the ship type you will need to type in the ships name up to 25
characters.
   Then you type in the Quadrant coordinates. The coordinates are numbered from
the top left corner. The X-Coordinate is going down, and the Y-Coordinate is
going across. The range is from 1 to whatever you typed in for Galaxy size.
   Next you type in the sector coordinates. They are exactly like the quadrant
coordinates, except for the range is always 1 to 10.
   You input the course (0-360), and then the velocity. The range on the
velocity is from 0 to the maxvelocity for that class of ship.
   Next you will enter the amount of fuel onboard. Range will be from 0 to the
max fuel capacity of the ship. You will do the same thing for energy next.
   If the ship has torpedo capacity, then you will input the number of
torpedoes onboard.
   You will repeat this for each ship in the game.
   After you have completed entering the data, the program chains to Star
Battles, and you will be listed as creator, with control of all fleets. Make
whatever changes are nessessary. You should check and make sure ships are not
about to collide with other ships or stars. You might also want to turn off
short and long range sensors so players won't be able to locate each other at
the start. You should end your turn at least once. The program will process
the turn, however ships will not move, nor will weapons be fired. After this
all you have to is SAVE your scenario. Don't forget to do this!
   Some tips: Planning goes a long way. You can print out the form I used to
plan my scenarios by typing at the DOS prompt: TYPE CREATE.DOC>PRN.
   I also found graph paper very handy for planning formations.
   Good luck! If you come up with some good scenarios send them to me, and I
will send you my latest scenarios. If I use them in a follow on scenario disk
I will give you credit for writing them.

